decisions:
  - id: d1
    decision: Ship v5.37.12 as security patch first, v5.38.0 architectural follow-up within the week
    rationale: 'Security''s non-negotiable bundle order: shipping Bug 1''s lenient parser before fail-closed semantics leaves fail-open paths live in the wild. Security auth-bypass concerns override scope-consolidation preferences.'
  - id: d2
    decision: loadPortalConfig returns discriminated union, unparseable fail-closes as violations
    rationale: The null-on-failure behavior was the root of every silent-drop scenario. Discriminated union forces callers to handle unparseable explicitly; unparseable → violations is the fail-closed contract.
  - id: d3
    decision: Error-message redaction — classifier strings only, never file contents
    rationale: Security audit flagged YAMLException.toString() and mark.buffer as data-exposure vectors that leak gate names / route paths into telemetry and LLM context. detail is a fixed English classifier, not derived from raw exception output.
  - id: d4
    decision: extractDeclaredGateNames throws on unrecognized gateConfig.gates shape, not silently degrades
    rationale: Silent degradation produced the numeric-index 'gate names' scenario. Loud throw propagates to MCP envelope so agents can see the failure. Matches the 'fail-closed contract' security required.
  - id: d5
    decision: __portal_unparseable__ sentinel value in usedButUndeclared, filtered from user-visible output in all four consumers (stop hook, compliance.ts, portal/check.ts, portal-check.ts)
    rationale: Reuses existing usedButUndeclaredCount>0 gatekeeper without new grep patterns in stop hook. Sentinel is detected and stripped at every user-visible surface — never rendered as a gate name.
  - id: d6
    decision: Duplicated classifyYamlError across paradigm-mcp and paradigm CLI for this release, extraction to portal-core deferred to v5.38.0
    rationale: Keeps the security-critical path self-contained; avoids introducing a new build-time dep direction from CLI → MCP during a hotfix. Drift risk tracked explicitly.
learnings:
  - Silent data loss is a security concern, not a reliability concern. When the data that's dropped is auth config, every swallowed exception is potentially an auth-bypass. The v5.37.11 prevention plan framing (silent no-op) was too narrow; the actual class is 'silent round-trip failure' — Jinx admitted this plainly in her reflection doc.
  - 'The bash || true wrapper at plugins/paradigm/scripts/paradigm-common.sh:515 was the widest fail-open hole in the system and invisible until security auditor explicitly traced subprocess exit code propagation. Lesson: wherever shell glues TypeScript error handling to user-facing effects, audit every silencing operator (|| true, 2>/dev/null, exit mask).'
  - When an audit adds a new scenario (Scenario D in this case) the architect's plan may need re-cutting before builder starts. The security-required bundle order reshaped a single v5.38.0 release into v5.37.12 + v5.38.0. Re-cutting inline (as I did) worked because all three specialists had already weighed in; in general, send re-cuts back to architect if scope changes materially.
  - 'Error-message redaction is an ongoing discipline, not a one-time fix. The security audit specifically called out that YAMLException.toString() embeds mark.buffer context lines containing raw file content. Rule: derive error strings from fixed English classifiers keyed on error properties (kind, reason, code), never from the exception''s stringified form.'
  - Duplicate-type fields across packages create drift risk. classifyYamlError now lives twice (paradigm-mcp + paradigm CLI). Short-term justified to avoid new build deps during a hotfix; long-term needs extraction to portal-core. Tracked explicitly in CHANGELOG and decisions.
